<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vasavadatta</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vasavadatta is a legendary princess of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pradyota">Chanda Pradyota</a> of Avanti. Stories tell how Udayana, king of Vatsa, was captured by her father, became her music teacher and eloped with her to his capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kausambi">Kaushambi</a>. The tale became one of the most popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> literature and is the subject of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhasa">Bhasa</a>'s play <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svapnavasavadatta">Svapnavasavadatta</a> and later works.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vasavadatta.jpg" alt="Vasavadatta" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rajasekharan Parameswaran · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VASAVADHATHA_-_OIL_PAINTING.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
